  <h2>What is barrel path?</h2>

  <p>
    Once the hands have begun moving toward the contact position, the barrel has
    one job.
  </p>

  <p>
    Arrive there efficiently.
  </p>

  <p>
    The beauty of an efficient barrel path is its simplicity.
  </p>

  <p>
    The hitter should not manipulate the barrel.
  </p>

  <p>
    The hitter should not manipulate the hands.
  </p>

  <p>
    The barrel is simply responding to everything that came before it.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the load prepared the body correctly...
  </p>

  <p>
    If the hitter remained balanced...
  </p>

  <p>
    If the body transferred energy through an efficient firing sequence...
  </p>

  <p class="emphasis">
    The barrel naturally falls into the hitting zone.
  </p>

  <p>
    It isn't forced there.
  </p>

  <p>
    It isn't steered there.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is delivered there.
  </p>

  <p>
    Imagine the bat as a rope being pulled by your hands.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rope doesn't decide where to go.
  </p>

  <p>
    It simply follows the movement that delivers it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The barrel works much the same way.
  </p>

  <p>
    When the body works efficiently, the barrel naturally enters the zone at
    the appropriate time, remains in the zone through contact, and only then do
    the hands fully release.
  </p>

  <p class="final-thought">
    Don't steer the barrel.
    <br />
    Build the swing that delivers it.
  </p>
</ChapterLayout>